use std
into webp_check.amni

Imports: os, gfx.codec_webp and gfx.gltf. A standalone program that checks the WebP decoder on the CPU and prints one line per check, then "webp: ok" when every check passed or "webp: FAILED" otherwise. The decoder already exists and must not be defined here: `codec_webp::decode(data, px)` takes a read-only byte slice and a growable byte list, gives width, height and a bool, and leaves px holding width times height times 4 RGBA bytes on success or empty on failure; `codec_webp::error()` gives the reason for the last failure as text. A module bool `failed` starts false; `check(name, ok, detail)` prints "PASS " or "FAIL " then the name, then " (" the detail ")" when the detail is not empty, and sets `failed` when ok is false. Time is always read as `os::clock_ns()` with the namespace written out.

`u32at(b, at)` reads a 32-bit little-endian unsigned number from a byte slice. `fnv(b)` is the 64-bit FNV-1a hash of a byte slice as a u64: start at 0xcbf29ce484222325, and for each byte exclusive-or it in, then multiply by 0x100000001b3 with wrapping u64 arithmetic. `hex16(v)` writes a u64 as 16 lowercase hex digits.

The cases file is the first program argument when one is given (`os::arg_count()` above 1, read with `os::arg(1, out)`), otherwise "hc/test/gfx/fixtures/webp_cases.bin". When `os::read_file` fails it checks "cases file" as false with the path as detail and stops. The file is a list of records, each: a name (32-bit length then bytes), the WebP file (32-bit length then bytes), the width and the height (32-bit each), the expected hash (8 bytes, a little-endian u64 of the FNV-1a hash of the reference RGBA), then a 32-bit length that is always 0 followed by that many bytes. Records are read until the end of the file into lists of offsets; a record that runs past the end fails the check "cases file is whole". A record's kind comes from the four bytes at 12 of its file: "VP8 " is lossy, "VP8X" lossy with alpha, "VP8L" lossless with alpha when bit 0x10 of the byte at 24 is set and lossless opaque otherwise.

`same(rec)` decodes the record's file and gives true only when the call gives true, the width and height match, px holds width times height times 4 bytes and its hash equals the expected one. It also fills a module text `last` with the first difference it found (the name, then "decode: " and `codec_webp::error()`, "size", or "hash").

W1: every record decodes the same. The detail is the passed count, " of ", the total and, when one fails, `last` for the first failing record.

W2: the same records split by kind: lossy, lossy with alpha, lossless and lossless with alpha, then by the size in the name: records whose name contains " 1x1 ", " 2x3 ", " 17x13 ", " 64x64 ", " 33x70 " or " 129x67 ". One check line per group with its passed and total counts; a group with no records fails.

W3: damaged files never crash and never give a broken result. For every record decode: the file cut short by one byte, the file cut to half its length, the file with its first byte changed to 0x55, a copy with the middle byte bitwise-xored with 0x5a, and a copy with every byte from the middle on set to 0xff. After each call that gives false px must be empty; after each that gives true px must hold width times height times 4 bytes. The bad-first-byte calls must give false. The detail is the number of damaged decodes tried.

W4: big pictures decode fast and exactly. Four 1024 by 768 files in the same folder as the cases file, each with its expected hash: "webp_big_lossy.webp" e96d3f73f75a7ffa, "webp_big_lossless.webp" 0a98e80c86ee464f, "webp_big_lossy_alpha.webp" 117a5a16faae1dc9, "webp_big_lossless_alpha.webp" 22f082db1dd576da. Each is decoded 7 times, timing every decode with `os::clock_ns()`; one check line per file whose detail is the fastest decode in milliseconds with one decimal and the speed in millions of pixels per second with one decimal; it passes when every decode gives true with size 1024 by 768, `hex16` of the hash equals the expected text and the speed is above 10 million pixels per second.

W5: glTF textures load the same. These already exist in namespace gltf and must not be defined here: `gltf::reset()` empties the loader; `gltf::load(path)` reads a .glb and gives an asset number, or -1 with the reason from `gltf::error()`; `gltf::textures` (records with an `image` field), `gltf::images` (records with `width` and `height` fields) and `gltf::image_pixels(i)` (read-only RGBA bytes of image i) are the loaded data. A helper `snapshot(path, out)` resets the loader, loads the file and appends to the growable byte list out, for every texture in order, its image's width and height as 32-bit little-endian numbers and then the image's pixels; it gives the texture count, or -1 after reporting the load error as a failed check. "scene_webp.glb" (textures that use EXT_texture_webp, one with a PNG fallback and one without) is compared against "scene_webp_ref.glb" (the same scene with PNG textures holding the reference decoder's output) from the cases file's folder: one check line passing when both load, both have 2 textures and the two byte lists are equal. The detail is the texture count and the byte count.

`main` runs W1, W2 and W3 when the cases file loads, then W4 and W5 always, and finally prints "webp: ok" or "webp: FAILED".
